WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.980
So with this film I feel like fashion
plays a big role in kind of how your

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.010
character goes on a journey like she
goes from fitted suits to kind of loose

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.910
trousers yes was that really important when
you were getting into the character yeah

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.960
the track the trousers that Juliet wears
when she gets to the island were very

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.770
important to me I was like I tried them
on I was like this has to happen.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.699
I hadn't, having done a couple of period
films, hadn't ever had the opportunity to

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:27.330
wear trousers so it felt really
important to me and they were also

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.410
trousers that Cate Blanchett once wore in
an old film because they're angels where

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.649
you go to try and all these costumes so
that was like this is like a good luck

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:41.910
omen but yeah you sort of feel that in
London she's she's sort of oppressed by

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:48.660
the war and her kind of sense of unease
and feeling very stuck and I

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.989
think when she gets the island she
really learns how to sort of release and

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:58.500
breathe and and trust in her own sort of
instincts really and I think that is

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.309
reflected by the what she was given that
it's island life and it's sunny and she

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:06.720
goes into these sort of more floaty
dresses and and into these trousers and

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.210
these jeans and it's great. It feels, as
romantic comedies go, it's actually quite

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.229
feminist and it's quite nice is this is
it quite, when you look back at kind of

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.920
the old kind of rom-coms or kind of
period dramas, is it quite refreshing to

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.390
play a role I mean you did Price and
Prejudice and Zombies which is ultimate feminist

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.869
but was it quite enjoyable to kind of have a
feminist rom-com? Yeah I mean I love

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:33.659
that that whilst this is a story that
and is ultimately she does fall in love

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:38.369
and it is about that and as well as but
it's also about this sort of finding a

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.140
family and friendship but alongside that
it's a woman that you know is she's

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.950
she's following her own path she's a
writer and and and and just as much as

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.700
sort of the fact that she does fall in
love there's this thing of like finding

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.049
her own voice and and and getting her
book out, finding her story and and

00:01:55.049 --> 00:02:02.759
fulfilling that as a sort of as a
creative person so and she makes all the

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:06.570
big choices you know she's the one that
asked him to marry her she sort of she

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:10.319
takes charge of her life in a way she
learns to do that and is rewarded for

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:13.860
that
which is important and it's told from

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.970
her perspective you know it's her story. And obviously when you're

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.080
used to film sets so there's lots of
makeup and you know at the end of the

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.200
day what's kind of your thing that you do
the minute you're kind of off set and

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:32.120
you're like I want to just go back to
being Lily? What do I do when I finish? I

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.120
don't you know I, when your location is
really nice because you end up just

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.270
usually just having like going to the
pub and having one like a little drink

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:44.430
just to calm down at the end of it day
and and actually here in corn - when we

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:50.670
filmed in Cornwall, Glen and I who
plays Mark we'd go on like quite big

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:55.440
long walks and and Jess and I did as
well walks along the seafront and that's

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.140
a really nice thing to do when you're
somewhere is to sort of really take in

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:07.500
where you are. I find by being by the sea
really calms me yeah. I think in this

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.830
movie like Dawsey he's an ally and
we're talking about allies these days

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.890
and how men can do that. What do you
think, what would be your advice to the

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.910
kind of men who want to be allies to
women, not just in Hollywood but like in

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:24.750
general? How can we be better? Dorsey he takes his time, he listens, he

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.760
doesn't invade in her space or try to
sort of control the situation he lets he

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.730
lets her be he lets they let themselves
it's like a conversation they find each

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:39.150
other they suss each other out there's
a lot of space and generosity it's

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:44.580
whereas with Mark as sort of he does
have his great qualities but it's

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:49.590
all sort of it's quite sort of invasive
and and and there's not much space for

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:54.570
her to sort of make her own choices
within the relationship and so she ends

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:58.470
up just sort of going along with it
because in some respects what he offers

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:06.330
is a sort of a viable choice but with
Dorsey he's got kind eyes and he listens.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:12.890
I think yeah I'd say that. So listen? Yeah, listen. Thank you so much.

